schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Centralization vs decentralization spectrum"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Centralized systems put one authority in charge of a resource or
    decision (a bank, a single telecom's mobile-money network, a handful of
    large farms); decentralized systems spread control across many
    independent parts that collaborate toward shared goals (cooperatives,
    rotating savings circles, seed banks, Bitcoin). Most real systems are
    hybrids somewhere on a spectrum between the two poles.
  source_traditions:
    - Bankless Academy's "Foundations of Decentralization" course
    - permaculture design principles (resilience through diversity)
    - food-systems resilience research (PMC 2022 study)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used as a foundations-layer bridge concept: reframes "decentralization"
    for an audience of gardeners and permaculturists by mapping it onto
    systems they already run (community gardens, seed swaps, cooperatives)
    before connecting it to blockchain/crypto examples like Bitcoin.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - technical/architectural decentralization (node count, consensus mechanism)
    - economic decentralization (distributed ownership of value/tokens)
  important_distinctions:
    - "the spectrum framing: most effective organizations are hybrids (e.g. a steering committee providing coordination while individual members make their own decisions), not purely one pole or the other"
    - "single point of failure is the article's named weakness of centralization; distributed resilience (the whole continues if one node fails) is its named strength for decentralization"
  tensions:
    - >-
      the article's cited 2022 study found decentralization improves
      supply-chain resilience but centralization can better address labor
      shortages — used to argue "decentralization is not always the answer,"
      not a categorical claim of superiority either way.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating decentralization as an unqualified good (or centralization as
    an unqualified failure mode) erases the article's explicit both-sided
    framing and its closing line: "neither is inherently better. The
    question is which approach fits the problem you are trying to solve."
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/centralized-vs-decentralized.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-a42af3f6559f
  source_lineage: centralized-vs-decentralized.md
